Pyramid Solitaire Release Notes

V1.1

This release is mainly a cosmetic upgrade for Pyramid Solitaire. Thanks
to some constructive criticism from Mark Griffith, I hope to have made
playing this game a little more enjoyable. First of all, I have canned
the Bell, except for error conditions, such as chosing an invalid move,
etc.

Second, I have removed the autofollow mouse and "wait" mouse position
cursor during card drawing and game processing. This makes the game
move faster and with more fluidity.

Third, I corrected the spelling of the word "Solitaire".

Lastly, I removed an include which was needlessly defining the MultiVue
Standard Menu structures. This saved about .5k in program size.


V1.2

This release has improvements which aid in the performance of the
multitasking system.

It also kills the group of get/put buffers, freeing up 4k of memory,
which previously hung around.

V1.3

This was another "cosmetic" improvement. It was brought to my attention
that if the MV user had changed the colors from the default with the
Control function, the game looked kinda weird. This was because the program
was assuming that Palette slots 0 through 2 still contained the default
colors. It no longer makes this assumption and sets up all four
palette slots for the appropriate colors.

V1.4

This release is mainly a "cleaning-up" release. There were still a few
places where the code would hog the cpu. These were cleaned up. Also,
general mouse interrupt handling was cleaned up. That is, the auto
follow mouse and the mouse pointer are turned off when not needed.
And some redundant code was removed which saved almost .5K in the
size of the binary!

I also fixed a "back door" bug in the program. This bug allowed you to
go through the deck again after getting a "Game Over" message by
attempting an invalid move after getting the "Game Over" message.
Actually, taking advantage of this bug only helped if and only if
you had missed a move from the Pile by not seeing it and clicking
on the Deck too quick.

Support for the CMP palette set is now also supported. This is
accomplished by a new screen at the beginning of the program which
asks you what kind of monitor you are using, RGB or CMP. Just
click on the appropriate word.

Oh, and all of you current and would be C programmers will rejoice in
that I have decided to include the source with this release. It is
in two parts, a .c file and a .h include file. I had to split it up
because I currently am using T/S Edit and it couldn't hold it all
at one time!! The code isn't commented very much, but it may help to
give you some insight on "how I did it".


Enjoy!
